WEBVTT

00:00:01.210 --> 00:00:02.270
<v Ira Glass>So, Valerie--
<v Ira Glass>那么，瓦莱丽——

00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:03.310
<v Valerie Kipnis>Yes.
<v Valerie Kipnis>是的。

00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:06.080
<v Ira Glass>You're a producer here on our show.
<v Ira Glass>你是我们节目的制作人。

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:06.990
<v Valerie Kipnis>I am.
<v Valerie Kipnis>我是。

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:11.740
<v Ira Glass>Your tone of voice betrays a lack of enthusiasm for the story you're about to tell me.
<v Ira Glass>你的语调泄露了你对接下来要讲的故事缺乏热情。

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:17.220
<v Valerie Kipnis>Yes. I do not find this funny. I find this deeply uncomfortable.
<v Valerie Kipnis>是的。我觉得这并不好笑。我觉得这非常令人不适。

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:30.090
<v Ira Glass>Valerie Kipnis is one of our younger and newer producers here. She's been working on the show for less than a year. And it was other staff members who urged her to come into the studio with me and tell me the story. Because they think it's funny, even if Valerie doesn't.
<v Ira Glass>瓦莱丽·基普尼斯是我们这里最年轻、最新的制作人之一。她在节目组工作还不到一年。是其他同事催促她和我一起进演播室讲这个故事。因为他们觉得这很有趣，尽管瓦莱丽并不这么认为。

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:31.960
<v Valerie Kipnis>OK, it takes place in a subway.
<v Valerie Kipnis>好吧，故事发生在地铁上。

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:33.260
<v Ira Glass>OK.
<v Ira Glass>好的。

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:39.230
<v Valerie Kipnis>OK, so I get on the subway and I see this person I know on the same subway train.
<v Valerie Kipnis>好的，我上了地铁，在同一列车上看到了一个我认识的人。

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:41.540
<v Ira Glass>Well, that's a coincidence. It doesn't happen very often.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，那真是个巧合。这种情况并不常见。

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:54.590
<v Valerie Kipnis>No. I've never seen this person on this stop before, no. And I'm like, I should say something to this person. And I'm like thinking in my head-- I'm like, all right, I should say something. I should say something. And then he looks at me. And he makes like direct eye contact.
<v Valerie Kipnis>没错。我以前从未在这个站见过这个人。我就想，我应该跟这个人打个招呼。我在心里琢磨——我想，好吧，我应该说点什么。我应该说点什么。然后他看向了我。他和我有了直接的眼神接触。

00:00:54.590 --> 00:01:06.490
<v Valerie Kipnis>And I'm like, right, this is my chance. I'm going to say something right now. And I open my mouth. And I'm like-- and then he starts looking at his phone. And I'm like, OK, that actually wasn't direct eye contact. That was like staring-through-you eye contact.
<v Valerie Kipnis>我就想，对，机会来了。我现在就要说点什么。我张开嘴，正要——然后他开始看手机。我就想，好吧，那其实不是直接的眼神接触。那是一种“看穿你”的眼神接触。

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:08.700
<v Ira Glass>How far are you from him?
<v Ira Glass>你离他有多远？

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:11.850
<v Valerie Kipnis>Six feet.
<v Valerie Kipnis>六英尺（约1.8米）。

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:19.610
<v Ira Glass>Six feet? It's so close, right, but so far. She's by the subway doors. He's at the pole in the middle of the aisle.
<v Ira Glass>六英尺？太近了，对吧，但也太远了。她站在地铁门边。他站在车厢中间的扶手杆旁。

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:36.910
<v Valerie Kipnis>So then, anyways, in my head, I'm like, OK, he'll probably get off on the next stop. It's not a big deal. He hasn't seen me. I'll just pretend I don't see him. But then, a few stops later, he has not gotten off. And he comes to stand right next to me, like shoulder to shoulder.
<v Valerie Kipnis>所以，不管怎样，我在心里想，好吧，他可能下一站就下车了。没关系的。他还没看到我。我就假装没看见他。但几站过后，他还没下车。他走过来站在我旁边，简直是肩并肩。

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:51.520
<v Valerie Kipnis>And I'm like, oh, my god. He's definitely noticed me. He's going to say something. I should say something back. And I'm looking. And he's just frantically scrolling on his phone, reading something really important, listening to something. I don't know. And I'm like, OK-- OK, I won't say anything again.
<v Valerie Kipnis>我就想，天哪。他肯定注意到我了。他要说点什么了。我也该回应点什么。我看着他，他只是在手机上疯狂滑动，读着什么很重要的东西，或者听着什么。我不知道。我就想，好吧——好吧，我还是什么都不说了。

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.420
<v Ira Glass>Because you don't want to interrupt him?
<v Ira Glass>因为你不想打断他？

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:57.040
<v Valerie Kipnis>Yeah, because I'm like, if he wanted to talk to me, he would say something.
<v Valerie Kipnis>对，因为我想，如果他想跟我说话，他会开口的。

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:10.470
<v Ira Glass>She keeps thinking, he'll get off at any minute. After all, he went to stand by the door, right by her. But he doesn't get off, not at the next station or the next or the next. And at some point she realizes--
<v Ira Glass>她一直以为他随时会下车。毕竟，他都走到车门边，站在她旁边了。但他没下车，下一站、再下一站、再下一站都没下。某一刻她意识到——

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:29.630
<v Valerie Kipnis>This is going to be a long ride. This isn't one or two stops. This is like a 35-minute subway ride where this person that I know very well is sitting next to me and I'm not saying anything. And our shoulders are next to one another's. And the subway is jolting. And I feel his shoulder on my shoulder. And I'm like, he's going to notice. But it's too late to say anything at this point.
<v Valerie Kipnis>这会是一段漫长的旅程。这不是一两站的事。这是一段长达35分钟的地铁行程，我非常熟悉的人坐在我旁边，而我却一言不发。我们的肩膀靠在一起。地铁在颠簸。我能感觉到他的肩膀贴着我的肩膀。我想，他一定会注意到的。但现在说什么都已经太晚了。

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:37.720
<v Ira Glass>Wait, wait, let me just pause on that. Because now the fact that you haven't said something makes it more awkward if suddenly you go, oh, it's you.
<v Ira Glass>等等，等等，让我停一下。因为现在你没打招呼这个事实，让你突然说“哦，是你啊”变得更尴尬了。

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:59.310
<v Valerie Kipnis>Exactly. But then it's-- in my head, I'm like, it's even more awkward if he turns to me and is like, has she been here the whole time and hasn't said anything? So in my head, I think this is a good idea. And I take out a magazine. And I flip it open as if I'm reading it. But I go like this.
<v Valerie Kipnis>正是如此。但在我脑海里，我想，如果他转过头问我：“她一直在那儿吗？怎么一声不吭？”那样会更尴尬。所以我觉得这主意不错。我拿出一本杂志。我把它翻开，假装在看。但我做了一个动作。

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:01.840
<v Ira Glass>So you're holding the magazine up to your face?
<v Ira Glass>你是把杂志举到脸前吗？

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.920
<v Valerie Kipnis>Uh-huh, so he won't see me.
<v Valerie Kipnis>嗯哼，这样他就看不见我了。

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:32.000
<v Ira Glass>Just so it's clear what we're describing here. Valerie is holding the magazine to her face so the top of the magazine is near her hairline. So she's like a nearly blind monk trying to squint at words in a Bible, or like an extra in an old musical where-- I don't know-- Sky Masterson or Auntie Mame or whoever is going to walk into a subway car. And everybody in the car has a magazine held up to his or her face so that they can pull them down on cue and start singing. The magazine is too close to her eyes to actually read it.
<v Ira Glass>为了说清楚我们描述的画面。瓦莱丽把杂志举在脸前，杂志顶部靠近发际线。她就像一个几乎失明的僧侣，试图眯着眼看圣经里的字，又或者像老音乐剧里的临时演员，不管是谁——斯凯·马斯特森还是玛姆姑妈——要走进地铁车厢。车厢里的每个人都举着杂志挡在脸前，好让他们能按预设的节奏放下杂志开始歌唱。杂志离她的眼睛太近了，根本没法读上面的字。

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:45.450
<v Valerie  Kipnis>No normal human reads magazines in this way. And the whole time I'm thinking, he's going to notice that I didn't say anything and why didn't I-- and I keep thinking, should I have just said something? I don't know.
<v Valerie  Kipnis>正常人绝不会这样看杂志。我一直在想，他会注意到我一直没说话吗？为什么我没说？我一直在想，我当时是不是应该打个招呼？我不知道。

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:49.560
<v Ira  Glass>Do you think it's possible that he's having the same experience you're having?
<v Ira  Glass>你觉得有没有可能他和你有着一样的经历？

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:50.540
<v Valerie Kipnis>Absolutely not.
<v Valerie Kipnis>绝对不可能。

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:52.100
<v Ira  Glass>Why?
<v Ira  Glass>为什么？

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:55.570
<v Valerie  Kipnis>I just know it.
<v Valerie  Kipnis>我就是知道。

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:56.200
<v Ira Glass>How?
<v Ira Glass>怎么知道？

00:03:56.200 --> 00:04:01.430
<v Valerie Kipnis>Because I don't think people feel-- I don't know. I don't think people feel this way. Do people feel this way?
<v Valerie Kipnis>因为我不觉得人们会——我不知道。我不觉得人们会有这种感觉。大家真的会有这种感觉吗？

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:03.790
<v Ira Glass>You're a person and you feel this way.
<v Ira Glass>你也是人，你就有这种感觉。

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:09.240
<v Valerie Kipnis>But, no, I don't think he notices me. Or if he does, this makes this so much worse.
<v Valerie Kipnis>但，不，我不觉得他注意到我了。或者如果他注意到了，那只会让情况更糟。

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:11.650
<v Ira Glass>Is this somebody who you're scared of?
<v Ira Glass>你怕这个人吗？

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:16.630
<v Valerie  Kipnis>No.
<v Valerie  Kipnis>不。

00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:18.360
<v Ira Glass>It sounds like you're scared of them.
<v Ira Glass>听起来你好像很怕他。

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:22.790
<v Valerie Kipnis>No, I'm not scared of them. But scared in a normal scared kind of way.
<v Valerie Kipnis>不，我不是怕他。只是那种普通意义上的“怕”吧。

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:24.860
<v Ira  Glass>What do you mean by that?
<v Ira  Glass>你这话是什么意思？

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:31.150
<v Valerie      Kipnis>Well, this guy's my boss.
<v Valerie      Kipnis>嗯，这家伙是我老板。

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:32.020
<v Ira Glass>Was it me?
<v Ira Glass>是我吗？

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:33.170
<v Valerie Kipnis>Yes.
<v Valerie Kipnis>是的。

00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:34.080
<v Ira Glass>Are you serious?
<v Ira Glass>你是认真的吗？

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.210
<v Valerie Kipnis>Yes, 100%.
<v Valerie Kipnis>是的，百分之百。

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:39.680
<v Ira Glass>[CHUCKLING] I absolutely did not see you.
<v Ira Glass>[轻笑] 我绝对没有看到你。

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:47.290
<v Valerie Kipnis>See? I knew it. But I was sweating underneath my winter jacket. It was one of the worst 40 minutes in my life.
<v Valerie Kipnis>你看？我就知道。但在冬装外套下，我一直在冒汗。那是我人生中最糟糕的40分钟之一。

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:51.010
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, I didn't see you. If I had seen you, I would have said "hello."
<v Ira Glass>是啊，我没看到你。如果我看到你，我肯定会打个招呼的。

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:54.260
<v Valerie Kipnis>But, OK, can I tell you another detail that's important?
<v Valerie Kipnis>但是，好吧，我能告诉你另一个重要的细节吗？

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:55.320
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>可以。

00:04:55.320 --> 00:05:12.340
<v Valerie Kipnis>This is so weird. You weren't wearing a suit. And in my head, I had never seen you without a suit. So I thought, maybe he doesn't want to be perceived. I was just like, maybe this is Ira's private time.
<v Valerie Kipnis>这太奇怪了。你没穿西装。在我印象里，我从没见过你不穿西装的样子。所以我当时想，也许他不想被认出来。我就觉得，这可能是艾拉的私人时间。

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:20.680
<v Ira Glass>So it was like seeing a teacher outside of the classroom when you're in elementary school? It just seemed wrong that you were seeing me there without my uniform on.
<v Ira Glass>所以这就像在小学时看到老师出现在教室外一样？看到我没穿“制服”出现在那儿，让你觉得很不适应。

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:27.830
<v Valerie Kipnis>Yes, yes, you were wearing sneakers. I'd never seen you wear sneakers. And I was like, I can't-- I can't say anything now.
<v Valerie Kipnis>是的，是的，你穿着运动鞋。我从没见过你穿运动鞋。我当时想，我——我现在肯定不能说话了。

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:34.540
<v Ira  Glass>What was I doing?
<v Ira  Glass>我当时在做什么？

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:36.840
<v Valerie Kipnis>Frantically scrolling on your phone.
<v Valerie Kipnis>在手机上疯狂滑动。

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:42.280
<v Ira Glass>When you say frantically scrolling through my phone, I was frantic for like a 30-minute subway ride?
<v Ira Glass>当你提到我在手机上疯狂滑动时，我真的在那30分钟的地铁行程里一直很疯狂吗？

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:51.740
<v Valerie Kipnis>I think you were actually reading something. It was not frantic in any way. But in my head, I was like, he's frantically trying to avoid me so he's scrolling.
<v Valerie Kipnis>我觉得你其实是在看什么东西。一点都不疯狂。但在我脑海里，我想，他是在拼命试图避开我，所以才在那儿划手机。

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:53.860
<v Ira Glass>Did you think I would get mad at you?
<v Ira Glass>你觉得我会生你的气吗？

00:05:53.860 --> 00:06:01.980
<v Valerie  Kipnis>No, but I thought you'd be annoyed. You'd be like, hey, I didn't say anything for a reason, Val.
<v Valerie  Kipnis>不，但我以为你会心烦。你会想，嘿，瓦尔，我没说话是有原因的。

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:05.360
<v Ira Glass>Can I ask you a personal question?
<v Ira Glass>我能问个私人问题吗？

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:07.730
<v Valerie Kipnis>Yeah.
<v Valerie Kipnis>可以。

00:06:07.730 --> 00:06:17.210
<v Ira Glass>Were you raised by people who didn't provide you a lot of reassurance that they liked you?
<v Ira Glass>你成长过程中，抚养你的人是不是很少给你“他们很喜欢你”这种安全感？

00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:24.590
<v Valerie  Kipnis>Yeah, I'm a Soviet immigrant, duh.
<v Valerie  Kipnis>是啊，我是苏联移民，废话。

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:45.890
<v Ira   Glass>It's kind of hard to get my head around it that she and I stood next to each other on a train for half an hour and our shoulders were knocking against each other and I didn't notice her and she said nothing and held a magazine to her face like a force field protecting herself and keeping us separate.
<v Ira   Glass>很难想象她和我竟在火车上肩并肩站了半小时，我们的肩膀撞在一起，我没发现她，而她什么也没说，还把杂志举在脸前像个保护罩，把自己保护起来，隔开了我们。

00:06:45.890 --> 00:06:47.880
<v Valerie Kipnis>Ira, can I ask this last question?
<v Valerie Kipnis>艾拉，能问最后一个问题吗？

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:48.870
<v Ira Glass>Mm-hm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:50.870
<v Valerie Kipnis>Why didn't you see me?
<v Valerie Kipnis>你为什么没看到我？

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:52.690
<v Ira Glass>Oh, I can totally tell you why I didn't see you.
<v Ira Glass>噢，我完全可以告诉你我为什么没看到你。

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:53.960
<v Valerie Kipnis>OK.
<v Valerie Kipnis>好的。

00:06:53.960 --> 00:07:15.080
<v Ira Glass>I just really can shut out the world really, really well and just decide to focus on something. And then in a work context, it's really handy. I can write anywhere. But in personal situations, all the time-- I don't know-- I can be in the same room as somebody and be thinking about something or focused on something.
<v Ira Glass>我真的能把外界彻底屏蔽掉，然后专注做某件事。在工作场合，这很有用。我可以在任何地方写作。但在私人场合，经常这样——我不知道——我可能和某人待在同一个房间里，却因为在思考或专注于某事。

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:25.580
<v Ira Glass>I totally shut them out. They'll say something to me and I don't even hear it, which is bad. And the people in my life don't love this. And this thing on the train is an example of it.
<v Ira Glass>我完全把他们隔绝了。他们会对我说些什么，我甚至都听不到，这很糟糕。我生活中的人并不喜欢这样。火车上的这件事就是一个例子。

00:07:25.580 --> 00:07:27.130
<v Valerie Kipnis>Yeah.
<v Valerie Kipnis>是的。

00:07:27.130 --> 00:07:27.860
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>确实。

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:29.890
<v Valerie Kipnis>It's funny. I feel the exact opposite in a bad way.
<v Valerie Kipnis>挺有意思的。我觉得正好相反，而且是往坏的方向。

00:07:29.890 --> 00:07:32.850
<v Ira  Glass>You're just always aware of others around you?
<v Ira  Glass>你总是时刻关注着周围的人？

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:41.880
<v Valerie Kipnis>Hyper aware to the point where I make up things maybe or I misperceive.
<v Valerie Kipnis>过度敏感，甚至到了我可能会凭空臆想或者产生误解的地步。

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:48.570
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. That seems hard.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。那挺累人的。

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:50.410
<v Valerie  Kipnis>Yeah.
<v Valerie  Kipnis>确实。

00:07:50.410 --> 00:08:06.740
<v Ira Glass>Today on our show, we have other stories of people who are so close, but so far. Act 1 is about two people in one of the closest relationships possible. They're engaged to be married. But they have this distance between them that they cannot make sense of.
<v Ira Glass>今天我们的节目里还有其他关于人们彼此靠得极近，却又极远的故事。第一幕关于一段极其亲密的关系中的两个人。他们已经订婚了。但他们之间存在着一种无法理解的距离。

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:23.100
<v Ira Glass>Act 2, comedian Tig Notaro has a story of somebody as close as her actual bedside, and also in another way, impossibly far away. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，喜剧演员蒂格·诺塔罗讲述了一个人，距离她近得就像在床边，但又在某种意义上遥不可及。来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:52.290
<v Ira Glass>Act 1, Love and Other Exports. How much can you trust whether somebody who you think is close to you really is close to you? Saidu Tejan-Thomas Jr. has been thinking about that question since visiting with some of his childhood friends. Saidu was born in Sierra Leone, but moved to the States when he was little. His story starts in Freetown during a recent trip home. Heads up, this story acknowledges the existence of sex.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，爱与出口。当你觉得某人离你很近时，你能在多大程度上相信他们真的与你亲近？塞杜·泰詹-托马斯一直在思考这个问题，自从他去拜访了一些童年好友之后。塞杜出生在塞拉利昂，但在很小的时候就搬到了美国。他的故事开始于最近一次回弗里敦的旅程。提示：本故事涉及性话题。

00:08:52.290 --> 00:09:10.840
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>When I left Sierra Leone at eight years old, I left behind a small group of friends I did everything with. As kids, we would run around the city and play soccer at the beach. That's how we bonded. Now that we're grown when I go back, we go to the gym. It's how we pick up where we left off.
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>当我八岁离开塞拉利昂时，我留下了一群一起做任何事的好友。小时候，我们会在城里到处跑，在沙滩上踢足球。那就是我们建立感情的方式。现在我们长大了，我回去的时候，我们就一起去健身房。这是我们延续过去关系的方式。

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:11.760
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>[GRUNTING]
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>[呼哧声]

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:15.620
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>My friends have this one particular gym they like. It's called Sonics.
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>朋友们有一个特别喜欢的健身房。叫Sonics。

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:24.730
<v Man>1, 2, 3, and 4, 5, more, 6, more, 7.
<v Man>1、2、3、4，再做5个，再做6个，7。

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:44.770
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>Sonics sits at the end of a dirt road in Freetown, a small space in a quaint green and yellow building. It has no air conditioning and relentless heat. So the guys are usually drenched in sweat like oiled-up extras in '90s R&B videos. I come here almost every time I'm in Salone.
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>Sonics位于弗里敦一条土路的尽头，是一栋古雅的绿黄色小房子里的一小块空间。这里没有空调，酷热难耐。所以男人们通常汗流浃背，就像90年代R&B音乐录影带里涂了油的临时演员。我几乎每次回塞拉利昂都会来这儿。

00:09:44.770 --> 00:10:01.990
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>But then the last time we went, I discovered that for many of the guys here, all their crunches and squats had a much deeper purpose than looking good. The gym, my friends told me, possesses a special luck for young Sierra Leonean men who want to move to America or Europe.
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>但上次我们去的时候，我发现对于这里的许多人来说，他们所有的仰卧起坐和深蹲，其目的远不止是为了好看。我的朋友告诉我，这家健身房对于想要移民到美国或欧洲的塞拉利昂年轻男性来说，拥有一种特殊的运气。

00:10:01.990 --> 00:10:19.230
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>They believe that if you use the weights here to sculpt your body, make it beautiful enough, you will find a wife overseas, and that Sonics has a track record to prove it. A member named Savage told me about how foolproof this method has been for previous members. He's speaking in Krio. I'll paraphrase after.
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>他们相信，如果你利用这里的器械雕塑身材，练得足够漂亮，你就能在海外找到妻子，而Sonics有成功案例为证。一位成员名叫萨维奇，他告诉我这个方法对以往的成员来说是多么万无一失。他用克里奥语说，稍后我会转述。

00:10:19.230 --> 00:10:26.620
<v Savage>We not ever get a situation where we get member, we meet with JC, then they normally come up together.
<v Savage>（克里奥语内容）

00:10:26.620 --> 00:10:32.240
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>We've never had a Sonics member who dates someone visiting from overseas who doesn't end up marrying them.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>我们从来没有出现过那种会员，只要和海外回来的人约会，最后没有结成婚的。

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:33.670
<v Savage>They may come up and then travel.
<v Savage>她们可能回来，然后就一起出国了。

00:10:33.670 --> 00:10:36.410
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>So you have a high success rate?
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>所以你们的成功率很高？

00:10:36.410 --> 00:10:39.780
<v Savage>Oh, yes. Oh, yes. We have barrage of them, barrage of them.
<v Savage>噢，是的。噢，是的。我们有大批这样的人，大批。

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:55.090
<v Saidu    Tejan-Thomas>Another member of Sonics, this guy with perfectly sculpted arms called Sultan, jumped in to name their legends, the guys they've heard found wives overseas, success stories that get passed around at the gym.
<v Saidu    Tejan-Thomas>Sonics的另一位成员，那个手臂肌肉练得非常完美、叫苏丹的人，插话列举了他们的传奇人物，那些他们听说在海外找到妻子的伙计们，这些在健身房里广为流传的成功故事。

00:10:55.090 --> 00:10:59.220
<v Sultan>Balance... Salam... Brasiliki...
<v Sultan>巴兰斯……萨拉姆……巴西利奇……

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:14.460
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>There was a driver who chauffeured a Black American woman around Freetown while she was visiting, always making sure to look presentable doing it. He made a good impression. They got married. And he just went to America a few months ago.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>有个司机，他在一位美国黑人女性来弗里敦旅游时负责开车，工作时总是表现得彬彬有礼。他留下了很好的印象。他们结婚了。几个月前他刚去了美国。

00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:16.530
<v Sultan>Scientis... Alpha...
<v Sultan>赛恩蒂斯……阿尔法……

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:34.070
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>There was the handsome photographer who got a random phone call from a Sierra Leonean woman living in London. It was a wrong number but he charmed his way into a conversation, sent her some photos of his biceps, and they got married last year. He's now living with her in London.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>有个帅气的摄影师，接到了一个住在伦敦的塞拉利昂女人的随机电话。是个拨错的号码，但他凭魅力开启了对话，给她发了几张二头肌照片，去年他们结婚了。他现在和她在伦敦生活。

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:37.890
<v Man>Next sets, next sets. Next, come on.
<v Man>下一组，下一组。下一位，快点。

00:11:37.890 --> 00:12:09.040
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>I respected the hustle. But their plans somehow didn't sit right with me. The idea of guys I grew up with, men in my country using their bodies to get overseas, it made me uncomfortable. But then I sat down with a few of the guys and we started to talk. And I realized beneath the surface, beyond all the muscles, confidence, and bravado, all of these men were carrying around a broken heart, which brings me to the guy I wanted to talk to the most, my friend Mark.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>我尊重这种拼劲。但他们的计划在某种程度上让我感到不安。我从小一起长大的朋友，我国家的人，利用自己的身体去海外，这让我感到不舒服。但后来我和其中几个人坐下来聊了聊。我意识到，在表面之下，在那所有的肌肉、自信和虚张声势背后，这些男人都带着一颗破碎的心。这让我想到了我最想交谈的一个人，我的朋友马克。

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:12.280
<v Man>MC Mark, Mario Mark, Mario Mark.
<v Man>MC马克，马里奥·马克，马里奥·马克。

00:12:12.280 --> 00:12:21.040
<v Mark>(SINGING) How do I breathe? How do I breathe?
<v Mark>（唱歌）我该如何呼吸？我该如何呼吸？

00:12:21.040 --> 00:12:36.850
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>Mark and I grew up in the same neighborhood. But we didn't really get to know each other until we were grown. He's a good-looking guy who's easy to talk to. Not the greatest singer, but we were hanging out one day and he decided to sing me his favorite love song.
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>马克和我在同一个社区长大。但直到我们成年，才真正熟络起来。他长得很帅，很好说话。歌唱得不是最好，但有一天我们在闲逛，他决定唱他最喜欢的爱情歌给我听。

00:12:36.850 --> 00:12:45.400
<v Mark>(SINGING) --feels so different being here. I was so used to being next to you. Life for me is not the same. There is no one to talk to.
<v Mark>（唱歌）……这里的一切感觉如此不同。我习惯了你在身边。我的生活已不再一样。无人可诉说。

00:12:45.400 --> 00:13:17.860
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>Mario's "How Do I Breathe," one of my favorite songs, is a song about a guy trying to piece together his emotional life after losing the woman he loves. It's basically Mark's story. It's why he's the one of my friends who believes most fervently that finding love overseas is the way to go. The girl he's singing about is someone he grew up with. Mark says she was the smartest kid in their fifth grade class. But she was always approachable. And he liked that. He liked that they could joke together.
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>马里奥的《我该如何呼吸》，我最喜欢的歌之一，讲述了一个男人在失去挚爱后试图拼凑起自己情感生活的故事。这基本上就是马克的经历。这就是为什么他是朋友中最坚信“在海外寻找爱情”就是出路的那一个。他歌里唱的那个女孩是他从小一起长大的。马克说她是五年级班上最聪明的孩子。但她总是很平易近人。他喜欢那一点。他喜欢他们能在一起开玩笑。

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:38.020
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>After lessons, they would go to the beach and talk for hours, getting to know each other. He says she was the first person who taught him how to love. Like this one day, he walked home from prayers and as soon as he opened the door, he saw her standing there with all his best friends from school, his family, everyone, singing.
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>放学后，他们会去海滩聊上几个小时，互相了解。他说她是第一个教他如何去爱的人。就像有一天，他从祈祷回来，一开门，就看到她站在那儿，和他学校所有的好朋友、家人，所有人在一起唱歌。

00:13:38.020 --> 00:13:47.810
<v Man>Happy birthday to you. Really surprised me. Come, you hug me. It's like, ah! Really, really happy-- I really, really-- I was just-- I just start crying.
<v Man>祝你生日快乐。真的给了我惊喜。过来，你拥抱我。就像，啊！真的，真的很高兴——我真的，真的——我只是——我只是开始哭。

00:13:47.810 --> 00:13:57.110
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>She'd planned this elaborate surprise party for his 14th birthday, complete with all his favorite food and all the people he loved. And on top of all that, she gave him a kiss.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>她为他14岁生日策划了这个精心设计的惊喜派对，准备了他最喜欢的食物和他爱的人。最重要的是，她给了他一个吻。

00:13:57.110 --> 00:14:00.490
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>Was it on your cheek or your mouth or your lips?
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>是脸颊上，还是嘴上，或者嘴唇上？

00:14:00.490 --> 00:14:08.730
<v Mark>On my lips, in front of everybody-- so in front of my mom, in front of [KRIO], in front of me grandpa, me granny.
<v Mark>在嘴唇上，当着所有人的面——当着我妈妈的面，当着……的面，当着爷爷奶奶的面。

00:14:08.730 --> 00:14:10.460
<v Mark>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mark>[笑声]

00:14:10.460 --> 00:14:14.100
<v Mark>The kiss was so special to me, than all the kiss that anyone would ever kiss me.
<v Mark>那个吻对我来说，比任何人的吻都要特别。

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:20.760
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>So that was the most special kiss that you've ever had? That was like the best-- that's the best kiss you've ever had?
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>所以那是你经历过最特别的吻？那是你经历过最好的吻？

00:14:20.760 --> 00:14:24.970
<v Mark>The best ever.
<v Mark>最好的，绝对是。

00:14:24.970 --> 00:14:37.050
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>Neither of them came from money, far from it. But Mark was growing up with a single parent. When his mother couldn't afford to care for him, he lived with his grandpa.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>他们两人家境都不好，远非如此。但马克是单亲家庭长大的。当妈妈负担不起照顾他的费用时，他寄住在爷爷家。

00:14:37.050 --> 00:14:55.600
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>But then his grandpa died. Then his mother died. And Mark became an orphan. He says his relationship with this woman was one of the few things in his life that felt stable. With her, he could dream. He could escape into one of their long talks about how sweet their lives would be in the future.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>但后来爷爷去世了。接着妈妈也去世了，马克成了孤儿。他说他与这个女孩的关系是他生活中为数不多感到稳定的事物之一。和她在一起，他可以做梦。他可以逃避到他们漫长的谈话中，谈论未来他们的生活会多么甜蜜。

00:14:55.600 --> 00:15:09.960
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>I reached out to her to get her side of this, but she didn't want to be involved with my story and she wouldn't confirm anything Mark said. The way Mark tells it though, they had decided-- she would work in the energy industry. He would become a lawyer.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>我试着联络她想听听她的版本，但她不想参与我的故事，也不愿意证实马克说的任何事。但按马克的说法，他们已经决定了——她会在能源行业工作。他会成为一名律师。

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:18.230
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>They wanted three to four kids, a house in Salone. Maybe they'd travel sometimes. I love the way he describes the love he had for her.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>他们想要三四个孩子，在塞拉利昂有个家。也许偶尔去旅行。我喜欢他描述他对她那种爱的方式。

00:15:18.230 --> 00:15:19.640
<v Mark>So real to me, no...
<v Mark>对我来说如此真实，不……

00:15:19.640 --> 00:15:22.310
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>She was so real to me.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>她对我来说太真实了。

00:15:22.310 --> 00:15:27.770
<v Mark>If I get or not get, e nor wan know how i dae or how i look lek, na love e bin get for me.
<v Mark>无论我有没有钱，她不在乎我怎么过怎么看，这就是她对我的爱。

00:15:27.770 --> 00:15:34.690
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>She didn't care if I had money or didn't have money. She didn't care how I looked. It's love she had for me.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>她不在乎我有没有钱。她不在乎我长得怎样。这是她对我的爱。

00:15:34.690 --> 00:15:37.330
<v Mark>True love from the bottom of her heart.
<v Mark>来自她内心深处的真爱。

00:15:37.330 --> 00:15:40.810
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>And you thought to yourself, this is going to be my wife.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>当时你心想，这就是我要娶的妻子。

00:15:40.810 --> 00:15:42.680
<v Mark>Yeah, she's going to be my wife in the future since then.
<v Mark>是的，从那时候起，我就认定她会是我未来的妻子。

00:15:42.680 --> 00:16:06.600
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>After they graduated high school, she got a scholarship to attend university abroad. Mark says she tried to put in a good word for him to get the same scholarship, but it didn't work out. Still, he encouraged her to go. And for three years, they continued their relationship via WhatsApp and Facebook. She told him she was one of the top students in her class.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>高中毕业后，她获得了去国外留学的奖学金。马克说她曾为他美言，希望能拿到同样的奖学金，但没成功。不过，他还是鼓励她去。那三年，他们通过WhatsApp和Facebook继续保持着恋爱关系。她告诉他她是班上顶尖的学生。

00:16:06.600 --> 00:16:34.280
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>Back home, Mark was struggling. He had no money, no parents, no connections in a country with no reliable jobs for young people. His dreams of becoming a lawyer were fading. But word of his girlfriend's success was starting to get around the wider Sierra Leonean community. Guys were sliding into her DMs on socials. And whenever that happened, she would tell Mark and then block them immediately, until someone new approached her.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>在国内，马克在挣扎。他没钱，没父母，在这个缺乏稳定就业机会的国家没有任何人脉。他成为律师的梦想正在破灭。但关于他女朋友成就的消息开始在更广泛的塞拉利昂社区传开。男人们在社交网络上不断发私信骚扰她。每当发生这种情况，她会告诉马克，然后立即拉黑他们，直到有新人再次接近。

00:16:34.280 --> 00:16:40.200
<v Mark>One time when I was sitting down, she told me that I have this guy.
<v Mark>有一次我坐着的时候，她告诉我，我这儿有个男的。

00:16:40.200 --> 00:17:06.870
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>The guy was a Sierra Leonean doctor. He had seen one picture of Mark's girlfriend and decided that she would be his wife. Mark says she tried to ignore him, but the man contacted her family and formally asked for her hand. She told Mark they were pressuring her to accept, telling her that a marriage with this man could not only change her future, but her entire family's too.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>那是个塞拉利昂医生。他看了马克女朋友的一张照片，就决定要娶她。马克说她试图无视他，但那个人联络了她的家人，正式提亲。她告诉马克，家人们在向她施压，说嫁给这个男人不仅能改变她的未来，也能改变她全家的未来。

00:17:06.870 --> 00:17:29.940
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>He watched as the nine years of their relationship unraveled over a short series of tearful WhatsApp calls. Nothing Mark could say, nothing he could do, nothing he could offer her or her family was enough to convince them to choose him over a doctor. A few months later, he says she received the ring via DHL and was married to this stranger.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>他眼睁睁看着九年的感情在几次充满泪水的WhatsApp通话中分崩离析。无论马克说什么，无论他做什么，无论他能为她或她的家人提供什么，都不足以让他们选择他而不是那个医生。几个月后，他说她收到了通过DHL寄来的戒指，嫁给了一个陌生人。

00:17:29.940 --> 00:17:54.430
<v Mark>A doctor who don't know where you are from, who doesn't know [SUCKS TEETH]. After all the plans, after all the promises, disappointment.
<v Mark>一个不知道你来自哪里、不知道……的医生。在所有的计划之后，在所有的承诺之后，只有失望。

00:17:54.430 --> 00:17:56.100
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>Love became disappointment?
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>爱变成了失望？

00:17:56.100 --> 00:18:02.300
<v Mark>Yes, it changes everything because she met somebody, a doctor. The moment will hurt me the most in my life.
<v Mark>是的，因为她遇到了别人，一个医生。这是我一生中最痛苦的时刻。

00:18:02.300 --> 00:18:42.850
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>The visionary feminist writer bell hooks says love is made up of seven parts. Wherever someone is practicing care, affection, recognition, respect, commitment, trust, and open, honest communication, there is love, even in an economically poor environment like Salone. But talking to Mark, I found myself thinking more about what love needs to survive, what conditions can make the act of loving easier or more difficult to sustain. Mark felt like he couldn't compete because of money. And he was right. Not everyone has equal access to love.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>远见卓识的女性主义作家贝尔·胡克斯说过，爱由七个部分组成。无论何处，只要有人在实践关怀、爱慕、认同、尊重、承诺、信任以及坦诚的沟通，爱就在那里，即使是在像塞拉利昂这样贫穷的环境中也是如此。但和马克聊天时，我发现自己更多地在思考爱需要什么才能生存，什么样的条件能让去爱的行为变得更容易或更难维持。马克觉得因为没钱，他没法竞争。他是对的。并非所有人都能平等地获得爱。

00:18:42.850 --> 00:18:49.190
<v Mark>I feel like crying. But it's shameful to cry before you. [LAUGHTER]
<v Mark>我感觉想哭。但在你面前哭很丢人。[笑声]

00:18:49.190 --> 00:18:53.670
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>You feel like crying, but you're ashamed to cry in front of me? You should cry!
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>你想哭，但不好意思在我面前哭？你应该哭！

00:18:53.670 --> 00:18:55.850
<v Mark>I miss those moments with her.
<v Mark>我怀念和她在一起的那些时光。

00:18:55.850 --> 00:19:26.610
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>In the years since I left Salone, I had fantasies about the lives of people back home. Our country had been ravaged by years of war that left a lot of heartache, suffering, and poverty in its wake. But I believe that through all of that, people had each other, that they had this unbreakable bond that was vital to our survival. But Mark was telling me that was wrong.
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>自从离开塞拉利昂后，我就对国内人们的生活有过幻想。我们的国家饱受战争摧残，留下了很多心碎、苦难和贫困。但我相信，通过这一切，人们还有彼此，他们拥有某种对我们生存至关重要的、牢不可破的纽带。但马克却告诉我那是错的。

00:19:26.610 --> 00:19:32.920
<v Mark>I don't think-- love is not here. If it is, it's at 10% or 5%.
<v Mark>我不认为……爱不在这个地方。如果有，也只有5%或10%。

00:19:32.920 --> 00:19:55.600
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>If it is, only 10% or 5% have true love. It wasn't just Mark. Almost every guy I talked to in Freetown told me the same thing. It felt like there was this lovelessness among the men in Salone. Every time I raised a question-- and I talked to around 18 or 19 people-- they'd launch into their own stories of heartbreak.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>如果真的有，只有5%或10%的人拥有真爱。不只是马克。我在弗里敦聊到的几乎每个人都告诉了我同样的事情。这感觉像是塞拉利昂的男人之间有一种“爱的缺失”。每当我提出这个问题——我聊了大约18或19个人——他们就会展开各自的心碎故事。

00:19:55.600 --> 00:20:12.950
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>I heard it from the taxi drivers, coconut salesmen, hotel bellhops, random guys on the street-- women too. The guys at Sonics gym said they'd all gone through it and independently landed on the same math. I asked them what percent of people without money in Salone find true love.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>我从出租车司机、卖椰子的小贩、酒店行李员、街上的随机路人那里都听到了——女人也一样。Sonics健身房的伙计们说他们都经历过，并且不约而同地得出了相同的结论。我问他们，在塞拉利昂，没钱的人里有多少比例的人能找到真爱。

00:20:12.950 --> 00:20:15.540
<v Man 1>What's the percent of true love without money in Salone?
<v Man 1>在塞拉利昂，没钱获得真爱的比例是多少？

00:20:15.540 --> 00:20:17.320
<v Man 2>It's 5%. 5%.
<v Man 2>是5%。5%。

00:20:17.320 --> 00:20:18.260
<v Man 3>5%
<v Man 3>5%。

00:20:18.260 --> 00:20:19.260
<v Man 4>5%.
<v Man 4>5%。

00:20:19.260 --> 00:20:20.510
<v Man 5>5%
<v Man 5>5%。

00:20:20.510 --> 00:20:21.350
<v Man 1>I say, yeah, sure.
<v Man 1>我说，啊，好吧。

00:20:21.350 --> 00:20:35.820
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>When I ask my friends in America the same question about finding love without money, they say 60% to 80%.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>当我问美国的那些朋友同样关于“没钱是否能找到真爱”的问题时，他们说是60%到80%。

00:20:35.820 --> 00:20:45.260
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>If love was like everything else these guys couldn't afford in Salone, if it really was a luxury, it made sense to me that they thought the best course of action was to look for it abroad.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>如果爱也像这些家伙在塞拉利昂买不起的一切一样，如果它真的是一种奢侈品，那么他们认为最好的出路就是去海外寻找，这对我来说就讲得通了。

00:20:45.260 --> 00:21:03.210
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>It was a way to solve two problems at once. They could move to a country where poverty was less rampant and true love was more accessible. But I wondered about it. Where would it lead them? For Mark, it led him to the place most of us turn to for finding love these days, the internet.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>这是一种能同时解决两个问题的方法。他们可以搬到一个贫困程度较低、真爱更容易获得的地方。但我很怀疑。这会把他们引向何方？对马克来说，这把他引向了我们现在大多数人寻找爱情的地方——互联网。

00:21:03.210 --> 00:21:12.250
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>Let me see the first message you sent this person. This one is really long.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>让我看看你发给这个人的第一条消息。这一条真的很长。

00:21:12.250 --> 00:21:12.820
<v Mark>Really long.
<v Mark>真的长。

00:21:12.820 --> 00:21:28.290
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>Mark got on Facebook. And he started sliding into as many DMs as possible. He didn't care about age or race or religion, just anyone who was over 18 and overseas who might be interested in a relationship.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>马克上了Facebook。他开始尽可能多地给陌生人发私信。他不关心年龄、种族或宗教，只要是18岁以上、在海外且可能对一段关系感兴趣的人就行。

00:21:28.290 --> 00:21:32.490
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>OK, so just tell me what you're saying in these messages. Just read them.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>好吧，那就告诉我你在这些消息里说了什么。读出来就行。

00:21:32.490 --> 00:21:48.640
<v Mark>Hello, Patricia-- Patricia, lover, how are you doing, dear? And she said "OK." I said, "OK, dear, it's good to hear." Then I said, "I am Mark. Nice meeting you, dear." Then she replied, "Same here."
<v Mark>你好，帕特里夏——帕特里夏，亲爱的，你好吗？她说“还行”。我说，“好的，亲爱的，很高兴听到这个。”然后我说，“我是马克。很高兴认识你，亲爱的。”然后她回复，“我也是。”

00:21:48.640 --> 00:21:56.640
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>So you're writing a lot. You're writing a lot. [LAUGHTER] You're writing a lot. She's only responding with like one word, two word answers.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>所以你写了很多。你写了很多。[笑声] 你写了很多。她只用了一两个词来回应你。

00:21:56.640 --> 00:21:57.170
<v Mark>Yeah.
<v Mark>是的。

00:21:57.170 --> 00:22:03.370
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>In his search for a new relationship, Mark messaged over 150 random people on Facebook.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>在寻找新恋情的过程中，马克给Facebook上超过150个随机对象发了消息。

00:22:03.370 --> 00:22:08.550
<v Mark>I said to her, Wilson, she's in America. Salma Amed America.
<v Mark>我对她说，威尔逊，她在美利坚。萨尔玛·阿梅德，美利坚。

00:22:08.550 --> 00:22:11.110
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>Some messaged back.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>有些人回复了。

00:22:11.110 --> 00:22:14.700
<v Mark>[KRIO] America, coming to country, Brazil.
<v Mark>（克里奥语）美利坚，回国，巴西。

00:22:14.700 --> 00:22:32.630
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>There was a single mom in Brazil who liked his muscles, the Sierra Leonean student in Sydney who talked to him because he reminded her of home, the nurse in London who thought he was good banter. Whenever he came across married women, he would keep it pushing. But he was hoping to connect with almost anyone overseas.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>有一个在巴西的单亲妈妈喜欢他的肌肉，一个在悉尼留学的塞拉利昂学生和他聊天是因为他让她想起了家，一个伦敦的护士觉得他很风趣。每当他遇到已婚女性，他就会继续前进。但他希望联络上海外的几乎任何人。

00:22:32.630 --> 00:22:46.640
<v Mark>It makes me feel good, like, talking to someone that is far away from me, like someone that is in America, you-- it would make me feel like-- I would just close my eyes. I feel like I'm in America.
<v Mark>这让我感觉很好，就像，和远方的人聊天，像是在美国的人……这会让我感觉——我会闭上眼睛。我觉得我就在美国。

00:22:46.640 --> 00:23:15.410
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>Listening to Mark, it was like I was watching an episode of 90 Day Fiancé, but seeing the side of the story I'd always wanted to see, the version where the hopes and dreams of the foreign spouse were finally center stage. My friend Mark was eagerly searching for a love that could transform his life. He made a few connections, but most women weren't interested in a long distance relationship with a guy all the way in West Africa. So Mark struck out again and again.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>听马克说话，就像在看一集《90天未婚夫》，但看到的是我一直想看到的那一面的故事——外国配偶的希望和梦想终于成为主角的版本。我的朋友马克急切地寻找着能改变他人生的爱。他确实建立了一些联系，但大多数女性对和一个远在西非的男人异地恋并不感兴趣。所以马克一次又一次地被拒。

00:23:15.410 --> 00:23:20.240
<v Mark>But one significant day I found Lottie.
<v Mark>但在一个重要的日子，我找到了洛蒂。

00:23:20.240 --> 00:23:22.120
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>He found Lottie.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>他找到了洛蒂。

00:23:22.120 --> 00:23:23.530
<v Mark>Lottie Rebecca Beasely.
<v Mark>洛蒂·丽贝卡·比斯利。

00:23:23.530 --> 00:23:25.700
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>Lottie Rebecca Beasley.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>洛蒂·丽贝卡·比斯利。

00:23:25.700 --> 00:23:35.630
<v Mark>She's my fiance-- she's my girlfriend. Because we don't get married to each other yet because of the distance. She's in America. I'm in Africa.
<v Mark>她是我的未婚妻——她是我的女朋友。因为距离的原因，我们还没结婚。她在美利坚。我在非洲。

00:23:35.630 --> 00:23:54.040
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>Lottie Rebecca Beasley was a Black woman Mark met through Facebook. And he just loved how regal her name sounded, Lottie Rebecca Beasley. You've got to say all three. She had kind eyes, a warm smile, and she lived in this state that Mark had never heard of.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>洛蒂·丽贝卡·比斯利是马克通过Facebook认识的一位黑人女性。他就是喜欢她名字听起来的那种高贵感，洛蒂·丽贝卡·比斯利。一定要把三个名字都念出来。她有仁慈的双眼，温暖的微笑，住在一个马克从未听说过的州。

00:23:54.040 --> 00:24:12.200
<v Mark>Fairbanks, Alaska, in the morning there, here it's midnight. 1:00, 2:00, 3:00 AM in the morning, I usually wake up just to talk to Lottie until 5:00 or 6:00 AM. It's all in the morning hours.
<v Mark>费尔班克斯，阿拉斯加，那里是早上，这里是午夜。凌晨1点、2点、3点，我通常醒来只是为了和洛蒂说话，直到凌晨5点或6点。全都是早晨的时间。

00:24:12.200 --> 00:24:18.430
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>But the nine hour time difference wasn't the only obstacle. There was another distance too.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>但九个小时的时差并不是唯一的障碍。还有另一个距离。

00:24:18.430 --> 00:24:28.560
<v Mark>She was born on 1949. She just turned 73 years of age this past September.
<v Mark>她出生于1949年。去年九月她刚满73岁。

00:24:28.560 --> 00:24:31.240
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>And did you know that before you clicked on her profile?
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>在你点开她的资料之前知道吗？

00:24:31.240 --> 00:24:32.610
<v Mark>No.
<v Mark>不知道。

00:24:32.610 --> 00:24:58.640
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>A 70-year-old woman wasn't what Mark expected. Most of the guys at the gym who had been successful had ended up with wives closer to their own age. But while Mark had been casting a wide net in search of a relationship that could change his life, he was also open to meeting a new friend. So he didn't overthink the age thing with Lottie. And she was calm about it too. In her very first message, she roasted him about how young he was.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>一个70多岁的女人并不是马克预期的。健身房里大多数成功的家伙最终找到的妻子年龄都更接近他们自己。但马克在撒网寻找能改变人生的恋情时，也愿意交新朋友。所以他没有在洛蒂的年龄问题上多想。她也很从容。在她的第一条消息里，她还嘲笑他还是个小屁孩。

00:24:58.640 --> 00:25:09.080
<v Mark>They jusy say hey, who are you, little boy? Who are you, little boy? What do you want? I said, I'm not a little boy. I'm a man.
<v Mark>他们只是说，嘿，你是谁，小屁孩？你是谁，小屁孩？你想要什么？我说，我不是小屁孩。我是个男人。

00:25:09.080 --> 00:25:12.460
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>How old were you when you met her?
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>你认识她的时候多大？

00:25:12.460 --> 00:25:16.100
<v Mark>21 or, 22, yeah, 21 or 22.
<v Mark>21或22岁，是的，21或22岁。

00:25:16.100 --> 00:25:44.670
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>Mark was young, but he's a good listener. Before long, Lottie was telling him all about her life. She'd gone through a lot of heartbreak too. She'd been married three times, had five children. And after one of her husbands had become abusive, she needed to get away. That's how she ended up in Alaska. She made the best of it, started her own business caring for the elderly, and was moving on with her life. She wanted to know about Mark too.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>马克年轻，但他是个很好的倾听者。没过多久，洛蒂就开始告诉他关于她生活的一切。她也经历了很多心碎。她结过三次婚，有五个孩子。在其中一个丈夫变得暴力后，她不得不离开。那就是她最终来到阿拉斯加的原因。她在那儿尽力生活，开了自己的店，照顾老人，并继续前行。她也想了解马克。

00:25:44.670 --> 00:25:59.080
<v Mark>What about my life? How I lost my father, I lost my mother. She said, oh, you're an orphan? I said, yes. She was having this sympathy for me. She was feeling sorry for me.
<v Mark>关于我的生活？我怎么失去了父亲，失去了母亲。她说，噢，你是孤儿？我说，是的。她对我产生了共鸣。她很同情我。

00:25:59.080 --> 00:26:16.650
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>For Mark, this thing that began as a friendship was starting to grow. He saw a potential for a romantic relationship. But Lottie struggled to see it, even though he felt like she wanted to. The age difference was too much. Mark says most people feel that way.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>对于马克，这段始于友谊的感情开始升温。他看到了恋爱关系的潜能。但洛蒂很难看到，尽管他觉得她也想要。年龄差距太大了。马克说大多数人都有这种感觉。

00:26:16.650 --> 00:26:21.500
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>So when you tell-- whenever you tell people about Lottie, about your relationship with this--
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>所以当你说——当你告诉人们关于洛蒂，关于你和这个——

00:26:21.500 --> 00:26:45.130
<v Mark>Don't believe. Don't believe. They all say, this is scam, this is scam. It's normal, though. People get their own thoughts and get their own thinking. And life, people must talk anything you do in this life. So if you live, if you stop by to listen to everyone, every word they say towards you, trust me, you won't even achieve your goal. Sometimes I'll give them deaf ears.
<v Mark>不相信。不相信。他们都说，这是骗局，这是骗局。不过这很正常。人们有自己的想法和思考方式。生活里，无论你做什么，人们总要说三道四。所以如果你活着，如果你停下来听每个人的话，听他们对你说的每一个字，相信我，你连目标都实现不了。有时候我会充耳不闻。

00:26:45.130 --> 00:27:02.250
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>I got to say, when Mark first told me about Lottie, I had questions too. Like I wondered, was my friend now a bona fide romance scammer or something? Because it is true that the way that he was looking for love gave him the silhouette of a romance scammer.
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>我得说，马克第一次告诉我洛蒂的事时，我也心存疑问。比如我好奇，我的朋友现在是不是一个名副其实的婚恋骗子什么的？因为他寻找爱情的方式确实带有婚恋骗子的影子。

00:27:02.250 --> 00:27:19.100
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>Scammers ask people questions about their personal life, send sweet messages. They try to earn trust before stealing your money and disappearing. But what Mark was telling me about his relationship made me believe that this wasn't that. Mark never asked for money. And he was putting in a lot of time with Lottie.
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>骗子会问别人的私生活问题，发送甜言蜜语。他们试图在骗钱并消失前赢得信任。但马克告诉我的关于他感情的事让我相信这不是那样。马克从没要过钱。而且他在洛蒂身上投入了大量时间。

00:27:19.100 --> 00:27:35.240
<v Mark>When she was in the hospital, day and night, I check on her. I said, I'm here. We're far in distance, but you are close to my heart. I said, I'm here praying for you day and night.
<v Mark>她在医院的时候，没日没夜，我去看望她。我说，我在这儿。我们的距离虽然远，但你离我的心很近。我说，我在这里没日没夜地为你祈祷。

00:27:35.240 --> 00:27:49.950
<v Mark>She had to accept me. Because of the way I talk to her, the way I care about her, do I call her when I woke up-- I wake up from sleep. I check on her. How are you doing? How's your health now?
<v Mark>她必须接受我。因为我和她交谈的方式，我关心她的方式，我醒来后会打给她——我睡醒了。我去看望她。你感觉怎么样？你的健康状况好点了吗？

00:27:49.950 --> 00:27:57.080
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>Months passed and Mark and Lottie kept talking, developing a love ethic where they both showed up for each other.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>几个月过去了，马克和洛蒂一直保持联络，发展出一种双方都为对方付出的爱的伦理。

00:27:57.080 --> 00:28:00.070
<v Mark>At one point, she actually said, did you eat breakfast this morning?
<v Mark>有一次，她真的说，你今天早晨吃早餐了吗？

00:28:00.070 --> 00:28:02.510
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>Did you breakfast this morning?
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>你今天早晨吃早餐了吗？

00:28:02.510 --> 00:28:04.620
<v Mark>I said, what is breakfast. [LAUGHTER]
<v Mark>我说，早餐是什么。[笑声]

00:28:04.620 --> 00:28:07.380
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>And I said, what is breakfast?
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>我说，早餐是什么？

00:28:07.380 --> 00:28:26.970
<v Mark>I said, OK. I said, no. I said, no. I said, please. I said, just understand. Here in Africa, our breakfast is for the politicians-- for the presidents, politicians, first lady. I said, sometimes when I wake up in the morning, I don't even taste food 'til 12:00 PM or 3 o'clock, sometimes even in the evening hours.
<v Mark>我说，好的。我说，没吃。我说，不。我说，请。我说，你就理解一下吧。在非洲这里，我们的早餐是属于政客的——属于总统、政客、第一夫人。我说，有时候我早上醒来，直到中午12点或3点都尝不到食物，有时候甚至要到晚上。

00:28:26.970 --> 00:28:51.290
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>Lottie asked about where Mark stayed. And he told her he shared one room with three other people. And most nights, he would have to stand outside the house to talk to her so he wouldn't disturb his roommates. This is common. Most people in Salone live on roughly $2 a day. Mark had been making a little money playing soccer in some local leagues, but it wasn't enough to get by.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>洛蒂问马克住在哪里。他告诉她他和一个房间里住着三个人。大多数晚上，他都得站在屋外和她说话，以免打扰室友。这很普遍。塞拉利昂大多数人每天生活费大约2美元。马克靠在当地联赛踢球赚点钱，但不足以维持生计。

00:28:51.290 --> 00:29:10.220
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>Hearing about how Mark was struggling made Lottie upset. So she decided to start sending him money. $200 covered rent for the entire year at a place all his own. She also paid for his grandma's funeral. It wasn't a huge amount, just what she could spare here and there, but it made her feel happy to help him.
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>听到马克的挣扎让洛蒂很难过。所以她决定开始给他寄钱。200美元就能支付他独居地方一整年的房租。她还资助了他奶奶的葬礼。数额不大，只是她偶尔能省下来的钱，但能帮到他让她感到快乐。

00:29:10.220 --> 00:29:34.090
<v Mark>So when she used to call me, Mark. I said, yeah, Lottie, yes, sweetheart. She said, did you eat breakfast? Yeah. Now is the time to have breakfast. Just laughing, ah, Lottie. When I close my eyes. I just think like she's in front of me.
<v Mark>所以当她过去打给我，马克。我说，在呢，洛蒂，是的，亲爱的。她说，你吃早餐了吗？对。现在是吃早餐的时候了。只是笑，啊，洛蒂。当我闭上眼睛。我就觉得她就在我面前。

00:29:34.090 --> 00:29:44.460
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>What do you think you were providing her? What do you think you were giving her? How were you contributing to the relationship?
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>你觉得你在提供给她什么？你觉得你在给予她什么？你对这段关系有什么贡献？

00:29:44.460 --> 00:29:53.930
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>Because she's contributing money. And she's contributing like financially. So what do you think you were contributing to the relationship?
<v Saidu  Tejan-Thomas>因为她在贡献钱。而且她在经济上做贡献。那你觉得你对这段关系有什么贡献？

00:29:53.930 --> 00:29:59.480
<v Mark>Love, love and caring for her.
<v Mark>爱，爱，以及对她的关怀。

00:29:59.480 --> 00:30:27.480
<v Saidu Tejan-Thomas>I can see that. A lot of relationships involve some kind of transaction. Each person brings what they can offer to the mix. With Lottie, it's money, at least partly. And with Mark, it's his time and care. When I think about the contours of their relationship, the fact that they've never met in person and live thousands of miles apart, not to mention the difference in age and experience, it makes me a little uneasy.
